1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,640
Ever caught a bright red patch on your eye in the mirror and had no idea it was even there?

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:10,160
That's usually a subconjunctival hemorrhage - a broken blood vessel under the clear surface of your eye.

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,880
It looks dramatic, but most of the time there's no pain and no change in your vision.

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:22,240
It happens when sudden pressure - what doctors call the Valsalva effect - pushes back into the fragile vessels around your eyes.

5
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:27,920
A hard cough, a sneeze, vomiting, heavy lifting, or straining on the toilet can all trigger it.

6
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:35,200
Rubbing itchy or tired eyes, or pressing too hard when you put in contact lenses, adds that same kind of pressure directly.

7
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:46,720
If you're on aspirin or a blood thinner, your blood doesn't clot the way it normally would, so even a small break bleeds more and takes longer to clear - and it tends to happen more often if you also have high blood pressure.

8
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:55,520
A cold compress for the first ten to fifteen minutes helps with any swelling, then switching to a warm compress after a few days speeds up reabsorption.

9
00:00:55,840 --> 00:01:03,520
For discomfort, reach for acetaminophen instead of aspirin, and never stop a prescribed blood thinner on your own - check with your doctor first.

10
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,440
Most cases clear up within one to three weeks without any treatment.

11
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:12,880
If you notice pain, blurred vision, or this keeps happening, it's worth getting checked by an eye doctor.
